      "worked": "Let $A$ be the tens digit and $B$ the units digit. The number is $10A+B$, with $1\\le A\\le9$ and $0\\le B\\le9$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}A+B&=\\frac{1}{5}(10A+B)\\\\5A+5B&=10A+B\\\\4B&=5A\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $4B$ is divisible by 5, $B$ is 0 or 5. If $B=0$, then $A=0$, which cannot be the tens digit of a two-digit number. Thus $B=5$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}4(5)&=5A\\\\A&=4\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe number is $45$. Check: $4+5=9=\\frac{45}{5}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
